VERDICT: FAIL
CHECKER: checker-second
FAMILY: adversarial
TASK: F3
ATTEMPT: 3
---
Formed my own view before reading checker-tests's verdict in depth; then read
it and independently re-ran/re-derived its central claims before deciding
where to attack further.

CONFIRMED (independently reproduced, not taken on trust):
- filepath.Walk's lexical order is a documented guarantee (`go doc
  path/filepath.Walk`: "The files are walked in lexical order, which makes
  the output deterministic"), so the `.a-broken.csv` fixture naming is not
  fragile / implementation-detail-dependent. Concern dismissed.
- Check 07 (nothing-decrypted guard) and check 08 (invalidateCache placement)
  both genuinely fail on their respective mutations when I applied the same
  mutations by hand on a fresh scratch copy (`/tmp/.../scratchpad/mut07`,
  `mut08`), not the oracle's copy.
- The blocking test (`TestRollbackDecryptionInvalidatesCacheBeforeReencrypt`)
  does not hold `s.mu` (it calls `rollbackDecryptionWithRecipient` directly,
  which takes no lock), package has no `t.Parallel`, so no cross-test
  contention. Ran it under `-race -run ...` on the mutated tree: fails
  deterministically in 12ms, no hang, no leaked-goroutine effect on other
  tests (t.Errorf not t.Fatal before `close(br.proceed)`). No soundness
  defect found here.

NEW FINDING NOT IN checker-tests's verdict, direction: undefended branch
(not a live bug today, but a real coverage hole in a confidentiality-critical
helper, and one that IS constructible where the disclosed "known gap" is not).

`rollbackDecryptionWithRecipient` has three failure branches: ReadFile fails,
encryptData fails, atomicWrite fails. Only the first (via a chmod-0000 file)
is exercised by any test. I mutated the other two to swallow their errors
(stop appending to `failed`) on a scratch copy and ran the full package
suite unmutated-elsewhere:

  $ cd mutEncData && go test -count=1 ./internal/services/storage/
    ok  budget2/internal/services/storage  59.5s   <- green with the
        encryptData-failure branch silently swallowed

  $ cd mutAtomic2 && go test -count=1 ./internal/services/storage/
    ok  budget2/internal/services/storage  59.5s   <- green with the
        atomicWrite-failure branch silently swallowed

Both are constructible, and trivially so, using tools already present in the
same file:
  - encryptData-failure: `failingRecipient{}` already exists at
    migration_cache_test.go:235 (used to test encryptFileWithRecipient's
    failure path). `s.rollbackDecryptionWithRecipient([]string{plain},
    failingRecipient{})` reaches the branch with zero new scaffolding. I
    wrote this as a throwaway test and confirmed: PASS on the correct tree
    (failed=[path]), FAIL on mutEncData (failed=[]).
  - atomicWrite-failure: chmod the containing directory 0555 before calling
    the helper directly (the same "directory, not path" technique the repo's
    own migration_atomic_test.go already documents and uses). Confirmed:
    PASS on the correct tree (failed=[path], file left plaintext, rename
    never happened), FAIL on mutAtomic2 (failed=[]).

This is a stronger finding than it looks next to the already-disclosed
"KNOWN GAP" in checker-tests's verdict, because that gap (DisableEncryption's
`if len(failed) > 0 { ...PLAINTEXT ON DISK... }` message branch) was argued
-- correctly, I checked the reasoning -- to be infeasible to construct
in-process, since decrypt and rollback of the same file go through the same
directory synchronously with no test hook between them: making the directory
unwritable early enough to fail the rollback also fails the initial decrypt.
That argument does NOT transfer to these two branches, because they don't
need to go through DisableEncryption's loop at all -- they can be hit by
calling the helper directly, exactly the way the existing
TestRollbackDecryptionWithRecipientReportsUnrestorableFiles test already
calls it for the ReadFile-failure branch. Nobody extended that same
direct-call pattern to the other two branches of the same four-branch loop,
despite the fixture for one of them (failingRecipient) sitting nine lines
away in the same file.

Severity, stated in the terms the brief asked for: this is the
undefended-but-correct kind, not the plaintext-survives-on-disk kind -- I
verified on the unmutated tree that production code handles both branches
correctly (reports the failed path, leaves the file's on-disk bytes
untouched). It is not today's live bug. But it is exactly the class that
sank attempt 2 ("two correct branches had no test behind them"), found in
the same function that attempt 3 was built specifically to defend against
that failure mode, in a helper whose own doc comment states its entire
purpose is "the caller must be told which files, if any, could not be
restored... nothing else in this codepath will ever say so." Two of its four
exit paths currently have nothing standing in front of that claim, and
unlike the disclosed gap, both were fixable with roughly five minutes of
work apiece and no new test infrastructure.

WHAT I TRIED AND DID NOT PURSUE FURTHER (given time budget):
- Full 20-minute oracle run: not re-run end-to-end (checker-tests's log plus
  my own reproduction of checks 07/08/01 in isolation was treated as
  sufficient corroboration of the mechanical claims; my attack surface was
  the coverage gap, not the oracle's arithmetic).
- Did not attempt to build an end-to-end DisableEncryption-level fixture for
  the disclosed PLAINTEXT ON DISK message branch; I agree with the existing
  reasoning that it requires an in-call timing seam the production code does
  not have. Not pursued further.
- Did not check provider.Recipient()-returns-error path (line ~140) for
  coverage; it's structurally required for the code to compile/run at all
  and is a low-value target.

Recommend: send back for two throwaway-sized additive tests defending the
encryptData- and atomicWrite-failure branches of rollbackDecryptionWithRecipient
(both fixtures already demonstrated above), or an explicit written
acknowledgment of the gap alongside the already-disclosed one if the boss
decides it's out of scope for this attempt.
